item_service_description:
&lt;p&gt;  Telephone transmission of EEGs has been most helpful in the following clinical situations:&lt;&sol;p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;  
&lt;li&gt;  Altered consciousness, such as stuporous, semicomatose, or comatose states;&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;  Atypical seizure variants in patients experiencing bizarre, distressing symptoms as seen with &quot;spike and wave stupor&quot; or other forms of seizure disorders;&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;  Diagnosis of a suspected intracranial tumor;&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;  Head injury, where a subdural hematoma may be identified;&lt;&sol;li&gt;

&lt;li&gt;  Headaches during the acute phase where, for instance, in migraine syndrome, abnormal responses may be seen.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  Telephonically transmitted EEGs should not be used for determining electrical inactivity (i.e., brain death), because of unavoidable signal interference.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
